1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,694
Look closely at this California
spiny lobster.

2
00:00:03,774 --> 00:00:07,432
Those long antennae and the spiny
shell are clues:

3
00:00:07,512 --> 00:00:13,301
Unlike the familiar clawed lobster,
this species has no big front pincers.

4
00:00:13,381 --> 00:00:18,710
NOAA filmed these animals among
the rocky reefs of California’s
Channel Islands.

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:24,225
By day, California spiny lobsters
often shelter in reef crevices.

6
00:00:24,305 --> 00:00:27,960
After dark, they emerge
to search for food.

7
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:30,312
They are not just scavengers:

8
00:00:30,392 --> 00:00:36,232
Their diet can include sea urchins,
small clams, mussels and worms.

9
00:00:36,312 --> 00:00:40,868
And when danger comes close,
a powerful flick of the tail

10
00:00:40,948 --> 00:00:44,114
can send a lobster swimming
backwards.

11
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:46,721
A hidden reef resident by day.

12
00:00:46,801 --> 00:00:52,562
A night-time forager after dark.
That is the California spiny lobster.
